154    H TYP=FUL$AUT=N. NICA$CIT=NDS 200, 2 (2025)$CUT=22-Aug-2022$             
154   C  TITL$NUCLEAR DATA SHEETS FOR A=154                                     
154   c  AUTH$N. NICA                                                           
154   c  INST$Cyclotron Institute, Texas A&M University,                        
154  #c       College Station, Texas 77843-3366 USA.                            
154  #c                                                                         
154  #c       On leave from the National Institute for Physics and Nuclear      
154  #c       Engineering "Horia Hulubei", Bucharest, Romania.                  
154  #c                                                                         
154   c  ABST$The experimental results published before Aug 2022 from the       
154  2c  various reaction and decay studies leading to nuclides of Z=56 to      
154  2c  Z=72, {+154}Ba, {+154}La, {+154}Ce, {+154}Pr, {+154}Nd, {+154}Pm,      
154  2c  {+154}Sm, {+154}Eu, {+154}Gd, {+154}Tb, {+154}Dy, {+154}Ho, {+154}Er,  
154  2c  {+154}Tm, {+154}Yb, {+154}Lu, {+154}Hf, in the A=154 mass chain have   
154  3c  been reviewed. These data are collected and presented in decay or      
154  3c  reaction datasets, together with adopted levels and gammas datasets    
154  3c  that are the most extensive collections of nuclear structure data for  
154  3c  each nuclide. This work is intended to supersede the previous          
154  3c  evaluation of the A=154 nuclides by C.W. Reich (2009Re14), which was   
154  3c  published in Nuclear Data Sheets 110, 2257 (2009).                     
154   c  COMM$As defined by 2009Re14, in this evaluation, the following         
154  2c  expression was used to define the rotational-band parameters A and B:  
154  2c  E(J)=E{-0} + A[J(J+1)-K{+2}] + B[J(J+1)-K{+2}]{+2},  with the          
154  3c  following terms added as needed for K=1 and K=2 bands:                 
154  4c  (-1){+(J+1)}A{-2}J(J+1), for K=1;  and (-1){+J}A{-4}(J-1)J(J+1)(J+2),  
154  5c  for K=2.   In the determination of the values of these parameters, the 
154  6c  energy spacings of only the lowest levels, and minimum number of       
154  7c  levels, were used.                                                     
154   c  CIT$NDS 200, 2 (2025)                                                  
154   c  CUT$Data available prior to Aug 22, 2022 have been considered.         
154   c  ACKN$The author is very grateful to the previous evaluators of this    
154  2c  mass chain, C.W. Reich (2009Re14), C.W.Reich, R.G. Helmer (1998Re22),  
154  2c  R.G. Helmer (1993He11, 1987He20), and B. Harmatz (1979Ha02).  The      
154  2c  author is also grateful to B. Pritychenko (BNL) and collaborators      
154  2c  (2011Pr03 and 2014Pr09), who contributed to the NSR reference database 
154  2c  work which made possible this evaluation, to B. Singh (McMaster        
154  2c  University) and the other compilers who prepared the XUNDL files used  
154  2c  in this evaluation, and to JoAnn Totans who helped retrieve rare       
154  2c  references.  I also thank the reviewer of this work and to E.A.        
154  2c  McCutchan (BNL) for the editorial work of the manuscript of this       
154  2c  evaluation, as well as to D. Brown, Head of the NNDC (BNL) during the  
154  2c  time when this work was completed. Finally I pay tribute to the memory 
154  2c  of T.W. Burrows, my mentor evaluator.                                  
154   c  FUND$This work is supported by the Office of Nuclear Physics, Office   
154  xc  of Science, U.S. Department of Energy under Grant No.                  
154  xc  DE-FG03-93ER40773                                                      

