154SM cL J$1993Zi05 give J|p=1+, from the reported excitation of this level via 
154SM2cL M1 radiation. However, this would require M2 for the 961.3 |g to the 3-
154SM3cL level at 1012. [This |g is not reported by 1993Zi05, but it is seen in 
154SM4cL the {+154}Pm |b{+-} decay (1.73 m).] From the reported |G|g{-0} and the
154SM5cL relative |g intensities, this M2 transition would have a transition    
154SM6cL probability |? 2000 times greater than that allowed by RUL. For the    
154SM7cL J|p=1- assignment, the 961.3 |g (now E2) would have B(E2)(W.u.)=23. For
154SM8cL a 2+ assignment, B(E2)(W.u.)=3.7 for the |g deexciting this level to   
154SM9cL the ground state and, thus, B(E2)(W.u.)=18 for its excitation from the 
154SMacL ground state. In the first instance (J|p=1-), the implied B(E2)(W.u.)  
154SMbcL for the 961.3 |g is large, but perhaps not impossibly so.              
154SMCcL In the second case (J|p=2+), the implied B(E2)(W.u.) for excitation    
154SMDcL      is of such a magnitude that the state probably should have        
154SMEcL      been seen in Coulomb excitation.                                  
