154YB2c       The studies were done on-line at the OASIS facility. The          
154YB3c       products were mass-separated and the A=154 mass fraction was      
154YB6c       transported to a counting area, collected on a tape and           
154YB8c       transported (within 65 ms) for spectroscopic study. A |DE-E       
154YB9c       particle telescope and a variety of Ge detectors were used to     
154YBKc       record coincidences. In addition, |g singles, |g(t), K x-ray      
154YBNc       and annihilation radiation were measured.                         
154YB c  1981Ho10:  Produced by bombardment of targets with {+58}Ni or          
154YB2c       {+107}Ag ions with energies of 4.4-5.9 MeV/mass unit. Products    
154YB3c       separated in velocity selector and |a measured with Si detector.  
154YB4c       {+154}Lu identified by growth and decay characteristic of         
154YB5c       its daughter.                                                     
154YB c  1984HaZD:  Produced by bombardment of targets of {+96}Mo and           
154YB2c       {+96}Ru with 250-260 MeV {+58}Ni and {+60}Ni ions. |g and X       
154YB3c       rays measured with Ge detectors.  Q values were determined        
154YB4c       from Kx/|b{++} ratios.                                            
154YB c  |a emission following this |e decay has been reported from levels      
154YB2c       between 3 and 6 MeV with I{-|a}=0.03% (1988Vi02). Similarly,      
154YB3c       proton emission has been reported from levels between 6.5 and     
154YB4c       9 MeV, with I{-p}=0.06% (1988Vi02).                               
154YB c  Data are from 1988Vi02, unless otherwise noted. Others: 1984HaZD,      
154YB2c       1981Ho10.                                                         
154YB c  Since Q(|e) |? 10 MeV and only a few |g's are reported and placed,     
154YB2c       the proposed decay scheme is clearly incomplete. Thus, it is      
154YB3c       not possible to quote reliable intensities for the |e+|b{++}      
154YB4c       transitions from these data.                                      
154YB cE TI        1988Vi02 show these values as upper limits only, since the   
154YB2cE      decay scheme is only fragmentary and, as a result, much of the    
154YB3cE      strength of the observed deexciting |g's may be from the          
154YB4cE      feeding of their parent levels by the |g decay of higher-lying,   
154YB5cE      but unreported, excited states.                                   
154YB cE TI(D)     1988Vi02 report significant |e+|b{++} feeding from {+154}Lu  
154YB2cE      to these two levels and from this conclude that J|p for the       
154YB3cE      {+154}Lu parent is (7+). They also indicate that this |e+|b{++}   
154YB4cE      feeding pattern suggests a similar structure for these two final  
154YB5cE      states. The odd proton and odd neutron in the {+154}Lu parent     
154YB6cE      state are in the |p h{-11/2} and |n f{-7/2} orbitals,             
154YB7cE      respectively. The implied low log{Ift} values indicate strong     
154YB8cE      Gamow-Teller transitions, which in this case would be             
154YB9cE      |p h{-11/2}|)|n h{-9/2}. It is now believed, however, that        
154YB0cE      the configurations of the two {+154}Yb final states are quite     
154YBAcE      different (see the discussion in the {+106}Cd({+54}Fe,|a2p|g)     
154YBBcE      data set, as well as those in the original studies (1993Zh10      
154YBCcE      and 1996Zh09)). Only one of these states, the (8+) level, can     
154YBDcE      be fed by a strong Gamow-Teller transition. The transition to     
154YBEcE      the (6+) level would be |p h{-11/2}|) |n f{-7/2}. It will be      
154YBFcE      much weaker (and, possibly, may not take place).                  
